    {
      "phrase": "exactly 1 more than the one before",
      "meaning": "Each step of a chain is a lookup: is x + 1 in the set? And if x − 1 is in it too, x is not where a chain starts, so skip it.",
      "pointsTo": "structure:hash_set"
    },
    {
      "phrase": "O(n) time",
      "meaning": "Sorting is O(n log n), so it is out. Starting a walk only at the first value of a chain means each value is visited at most twice: O(n) in total.",
      "pointsTo": "hashing"
    },
    {
      "phrase": "counts once",
      "meaning": "Building a set drops the repeats before you start.",
      "pointsTo": "toolkit:set_ops"
    }
  ],
  "constraintReading": "len(nums) can reach 10⁵, and the required O(n) time rules out sorting, which is O(n log n). Values reach ±10⁹, far too spread out for an array indexed by value, so use a set: O(1) lookups for O(n) space. Walking up only from the first value of each chain keeps the total work O(n).",
  "hints": {
    "clarify": "Do the chain's values have to sit next to each other in the list? No: only the values matter, not where they are. Try [10, 3, 11, 2, 4] by hand: 2, 3 and 4 are all there, so that chain has length 3, while 10 and 11 make a chain of 2. What about [2, 2, 3]? The repeated 2 counts once, so the answer is 2.",
    "approach": "Hash set for O(1) lookups. Put every value in a set. A chain is only worth walking from its smallest value: x starts a chain exactly when x − 1 is not in the set. From each start, count upward, x + 1, x + 2, ..., while the next value is in the set, and keep the longest count. Values in the middle of a chain are skipped at once, so every value is stepped over at most twice.",
    "whyNot": "Sorting puts each chain in order, and one scan then finds the longest, but the sort is O(n log n) and the problem asks for O(n). Counting upward from every value, not just from chain starts, walks a long chain again from each of its values: O(n²) for one chain of n values.",
    "slots": {
      "setup": "A set `values` of every number in `nums`, which also drops the repeats, and a best length of 0.",
      "loop": "Visit each distinct value x in the set once.",
      "update": "From a chain start, keep a running length and grow it while x + length is in the set.",
      "record": "Look up x − 1 first. If it's in the set, x sits inside a chain that starts lower, so skip it. Otherwise walk the chain, then keep its length if it beats the best.",
      "return": "Return the best length after every value has had its turn. An empty list gives an empty set, so the answer stays 0."
    }
  },
  "solution": {
    "code": "class Solution:\n    def longestConsecutive(self, nums: list[int]) -> int:\n        values = set(nums)\n        best = 0\n        for x in values:\n            if x - 1 in values:  # viz:check\n                continue\n            length = 1  # viz:start\n            while x + length in values:\n                length += 1  # viz:extend\n            best = max(best, length)  # viz:record\n        return best  # viz:done\n",
    "explanation": "Building the set takes O(n). The `for` loop visits each distinct value once, and the `while` loop only runs from a chain's first value, so it steps over each value at most once across all chains: O(n) time in total, even with a loop inside a loop. The set holds up to n values: O(n) space.",
    "toolkit": ["set_ops"]
  },
